
import java.util.*;
class Main 
{
    public static void main(String[] args) 
    {
        Employee emp = new Employee();
        System.out.println(emp.getsalary());
        emp.setsalary(30000);
        System.out.println(emp.getsalary());
    }
}
class Employee 
{
    private int salary = 20000;  
    public int getsalary() 
    {
        return salary;
    }
    public void setsalary(int salary)
    {
        this.salary=salary;
    }
}


explaination:-
“Encapsulation is one of the four pillars of object-oriented programming.
In this program, I have created an Employee class with a private variable called salary, initialized to 20000.
Since salary is private, I cannot access it directly from outside the Employee class. So, I use a getter method called getsalary() to retrieve the salary and a setter method called setsalary() to update it.
Initially, the salary is 20000. When I call setsalary(30000), the salary is updated to 30000. Then, I call getsalary() again to display the updated salary.
The output is 20000 and 30000.
This demonstrates encapsulation because the data is private and accessed or modified through methods.”
